# Instrument architecture

## Core rule

Every studio, trainer, and practice tool is implemented **once**.

Instrument differences come from the shared instrument configuration system
(`src/lib/instrument/*` + `InstrumentProvider`). Do **not** create parallel
instrument-specific feature trees.

| Do | Do not |
|----|--------|
| `PieceStudio` | `ViolinPieceStudio` / `ViolaPieceStudio` |
| `ScaleStudio` | `ViolinScaleStudio` / `ViolaScaleStudio` |
| `Tuner` / Note trainer / future `RhythmStudio` | Per-instrument copies of the same feature |

A UI, animation, UX, or logic improvement to a studio is made **once** and
applies to every supported instrument automatically.

Shared **chrome** (recording, viewport, coach bubble) follows the same rule:
one `MusaiMicCapturePanel` / `MusaiCaptureDock`, not a copy per studio. See
`.cursor/rules/shared-studio-capture.mdc`.

## Data flow

```
Feature (Piece / Scale / Tuner / …)
        ↓
useInstrument() / InstrumentProfile helpers
        ↓
Violin · Viola · future instruments (profiles only)
```

## Put instrument specifics in profiles / helpers

Prefer central config and helpers for:

- strings, tuning, open-string layout
- primary clef and notation rules
- playable pitch range / Hz window
- scale registers and expected pitch sequences
- instrument name, coach role, method-book hints
- playback / soundfont hints when tied to the **player** instrument
- any genuine musical constraints

Feature UI and flow code should **consume** this data, not hard-code violin or viola.

## Avoid scattered instrument branches

Do **not** sprinkle UI with:

```ts
if (instrument === "viola") { … }
if (instrument.id === "violin") { … }
```

If behaviour must differ, prefer:

1. fields on `InstrumentProfile`
2. reusable helpers under `src/lib/instrument/`
3. small strategy modules keyed by capability — not by copying the whole feature

Keep instrument logic out of presentation components whenever practical.

## Adding a new feature

Example: Rhythm Studio

1. Build **one** `RhythmStudio` (one route, one view tree)
2. Read `useInstrument()` (or pass `InstrumentProfile` into pure libs)
3. Persist `instrumentId` on sessions / progress keys when needed
4. Pass pitch windows into analysis explicitly — do not rely on violin defaults in shared analyzers
5. Extend profiles/helpers if the feature needs new instrument fields

## Adding a new instrument

Primarily:

1. Extend `InstrumentId` + catalog
2. Add a profile (strings, clef, range, scale defaults, coach copy, …)
3. Add only rules that cannot be derived from the profile (e.g. fingering)

Do **not** rebuild every studio for the new instrument.

## Existing naming debt (do not make worse)

Some shared modules still have violin-branded names (`ViolinTuner`, `ScaleTrebleStaff`,
`violinTuner.ts`) but are already instrument-agnostic. Prefer renaming toward
neutral names when touching those files; never fork a Viola copy instead.

Piece Studio playback timbre (`piano` | `violin` soundfont) is **not** the same
as Settings player instrument — do not conflate the two IDs.

## Checklist before shipping feature work

- [ ] One implementation path for all instruments
- [ ] No new `ViolinX` / `ViolaX` feature components
- [ ] Instrument differences live in profile or `src/lib/instrument` helpers
- [ ] Analysis / detection receives profile pitch bounds when relevant
- [ ] Progress / session keys include `instrumentId` when history is per-instrument
